w: 1.~Sing loud to God our strength; _ with _ joy to Ja- cob's God do sing. _ Take up a _ psalm, the pleas- _ ant harp, tim- brel and _ psal- t'ry bring.
w: 2.~Blow trum- pets at new\-- moon, _ what _ day our feast ap- point- ed is: _ For charge to _ Is- r'el, and _ a law of Ja- cob's _ God was this.
w: * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * * *
w: 4.~His shoul- der I from bur- _ dens _ took, his hands from pots did free. _ Thou didst in _ trou- ble on _ me call, and I de- _ liv- ered thee:
w: 5.~In sec- ret place of thun- _ de- _ ring I did thee an- swer make; _ And at the _ streams of Me- _ ri- bah of thee a _ proof did take.
w: 6.~O thou, my peo- ple, give _ an _ ear, I'll te- sti- fy to thee; _ To thee, O _ Is- r'el, if _ thou wilt but hear- ken _ un- to me.
w: 7.~In midst of thee there shall _ not _ be a- ny strange god at all; _ Nor un- to _ a- ny god _ un- known thou bow- ing _ down shalt fall.
w: 8.~I am the Lord thy God, _ which _ did from E- gypt land thee guide; _ I'll fill thy _ mouth a- bun- _ dant- ly, do thou it _ o- pen wide.
w: 9.~But yet my peo- ple to _ my _ voice would not at- ten- tive be; _ And ev'n my _ cho- sen Is- _ ra- el he would have _ none of me.
w: 10.~So to the lust of their _ own _ hearts I them de- liv- er- èd; _ And then in _ coun- sels of _ their own they vain- ly _ wand- er- èd.
w: 11.~O that my peo- ple had _ me _ heard, Is- r'el my ways had chose! _ I had their _ en'- mies soon _ sub- dued, my hand turned _ on their foes.
w: 12.~The ha- ters of the Lord _ to _ him sub- mis- sion should have feigned; _ But as for _ them, their time _ should have for ev- er- _ more re- mained.
w: 13.~He should have al- so fed _ them _ with the fi- nest of the wheat; _ Of hon- ey _ from the rock _ thy fill I should have _ made thee eat.
